T340	Quotation 69313 69519	"There, there, now, don't begin that groaning again. Open your mouth. Well-- your tooth IS loose, but you're not going to die about that. Mary, get me a silk thread, and a chunk of fire out of the kitchen."
R419	Addressee Arg1:T335 Arg2:T340	
T341	Entity 68510 68514	Mary
R420	Addressee Arg1:T341 Arg2:T340	
R421	Speaker Arg1:T332 Arg2:T340	
T342	Entity 69520 69523	Tom
T343	Cue 69524 69528	said
T344	Quotation 69530 69691	"Oh, please, auntie, don't pull it out. It don't hurt any more. I wish I may never stir if it does. Please don't, auntie. I don't want to stay home from school."
R422	Speaker Arg1:T342 Arg2:T344	
R423	Cueing Arg1:T343 Arg2:T344	
R424	Addressee Arg1:T332 Arg2:T344	
T345	Quotation 69692 69923	"Oh, you don't, don't you? So all this row was because you thought you'd get to stay home from school and go a-fishing? Tom, Tom, I love you so, and you seem to try every way you can to break my old heart with your outrageousness."
R425	Addressee Arg1:T342 Arg2:T345	
R426	Speaker Arg1:T332 Arg2:T345	
T346	Entity 70790 70801	another boy
T347	Cue 70802 70806	said
T348	Quotation 70808 70822	"Sour grapes!"
T349	Entity 70827 70829	he
R427	Cueing Arg1:T347 Arg2:T348	
R428	Speaker Arg1:T346 Arg2:T348	
R429	Addressee Arg1:T349 Arg2:T348	
T350	Entity 72573 72576	Tom
T351	Cue 72577 72583	hailed
T352	Entity 72584 72604	the romantic outcast
T353	Quotation 72606 72627	"Hello, Huckleberry!"
R430	Speaker Arg1:T350 Arg2:T353	
R431	Cueing Arg1:T351 Arg2:T353	
R432	Addressee Arg1:T352 Arg2:T353	
T354	Quotation 72628 72670	"Hello yourself, and see how you like it."
R433	Speaker Arg1:T352 Arg2:T354	
R434	Addressee Arg1:T350 Arg2:T354	
T355	Quotation 72671 72693	"What's that you got?"
R435	Speaker Arg1:T350 Arg2:T355	
R436	Addressee Arg1:T352 Arg2:T355	
T356	Quotation 72694 72705	"Dead cat."
R437	Addressee Arg1:T350 Arg2:T356	
R438	Speaker Arg1:T352 Arg2:T356	
T357	Quotation 72706 72772	"Lemme see him, Huck. My, he's pretty stiff. Where'd you get him?"
R439	Addressee Arg1:T352 Arg2:T357	
R440	Speaker Arg1:T350 Arg2:T357	
T358	Quotation 72773 72798	"Bought him off'n a boy."
R441	Addressee Arg1:T350 Arg2:T358	
R442	Speaker Arg1:T352 Arg2:T358	
T359	Quotation 72799 72819	"What did you give?"
R443	Speaker Arg1:T350 Arg2:T359	
R444	Addressee Arg1:T352 Arg2:T359	
T360	Quotation 72820 72891	"I give a blue ticket and a bladder that I got at the slaughter-house."
R445	Speaker Arg1:T352 Arg2:T360	
R446	Addressee Arg1:T350 Arg2:T360	
T361	Quotation 72892 72926	"Where'd you get the blue ticket?"
R447	Speaker Arg1:T350 Arg2:T361	
R448	Addressee Arg1:T352 Arg2:T361	
T362	Quotation 72927 72987	"Bought it off'n Ben Rogers two weeks ago for a hoop-stick."
R449	Speaker Arg1:T352 Arg2:T362	
R450	Addressee Arg1:T350 Arg2:T362	
T363	Quotation 72988 73029	"Say-- what is dead cats good for, Huck?"
R451	Speaker Arg1:T350 Arg2:T363	
R452	Addressee Arg1:T352 Arg2:T363	
T364	Quotation 73030 73058	"Good for? Cure warts with."
R453	Speaker Arg1:T352 Arg2:T364	
R454	Addressee Arg1:T350 Arg2:T364	
T365	Quotation 73059 73108	"No! Is that so? I know something that's better."
R455	Speaker Arg1:T350 Arg2:T365	
R456	Addressee Arg1:T352 Arg2:T365	
T366	Quotation 73109 73139	"I bet you don't. What is it?"
R457	Speaker Arg1:T352 Arg2:T366	
R458	Addressee Arg1:T350 Arg2:T366	
T367	Quotation 73140 73159	"Why, spunk-water."
R459	Speaker Arg1:T350 Arg2:T367	
R460	Addressee Arg1:T352 Arg2:T367	
T368	Quotation 73160 73214	"Spunk-water! I wouldn't give a dern for spunk-water."
R461	Addressee Arg1:T350 Arg2:T368	
R462	Speaker Arg1:T352 Arg2:T368	
T369	Quotation 73215 73263	"You wouldn't, wouldn't you? D'you ever try it?"
R463	Addressee Arg1:T352 Arg2:T369	
R464	Speaker Arg1:T350 Arg2:T369	
T370	Quotation 73264 73299	"No, I hain't. But Bob Tanner did."
R465	Speaker Arg1:T350 Arg2:T370	
R466	Addressee Arg1:T352 Arg2:T370	
T371	Quotation 73300 73318	"Who told you so!"
R467	Speaker Arg1:T352 Arg2:T371	
R468	Addressee Arg1:T350 Arg2:T371	
T372	Quotation 73319 73487	"Why, he told Jeff Thatcher, and Jeff told Johnny Baker, and Johnny told Jim Hollis, and Jim told Ben Rogers, and Ben told a nigger, and the nigger told me. There now!"
R469	Speaker Arg1:T350 Arg2:T372	
R470	Addressee Arg1:T352 Arg2:T372	
T373	Quotation 73488 73670	"Well, what of it? They'll all lie. Leastways all but the nigger. I don't know HIM. But I never see a nigger that WOULDN'T lie. Shucks! Now you tell me how Bob Tanner done it, Huck."
R471	Speaker Arg1:T352 Arg2:T373	
R472	Addressee Arg1:T350 Arg2:T373	
T374	Quotation 73671 73749	"Why, he took and dipped his hand in a rotten stump where the rain-water was."
R473	Speaker Arg1:T350 Arg2:T374	
R474	Addressee Arg1:T352 Arg2:T374	
T375	Quotation 73750 73767	"In the daytime?"
R475	Speaker Arg1:T352 Arg2:T375	
R476	Addressee Arg1:T350 Arg2:T375	
T376	Quotation 73768 73780	"Certainly."
R477	Speaker Arg1:T350 Arg2:T376	
R478	Addressee Arg1:T352 Arg2:T376	
T377	Quotation 73781 73810	"With his face to the stump?"
T378	Quotation 73811 73836	"Yes. Least I reckon so."
R479	Speaker Arg1:T352 Arg2:T377	
R480	Addressee Arg1:T350 Arg2:T377	
R481	Addressee Arg1:T352 Arg2:T378	
R482	Speaker Arg1:T350 Arg2:T378	
T379	Quotation 73837 73859	"Did he say anything?"
R483	Speaker Arg1:T352 Arg2:T379	
R484	Addressee Arg1:T350 Arg2:T379	
T380	Quotation 73860 73898	"I don't reckon he did. I don't know."
R485	Speaker Arg1:T350 Arg2:T380	
R486	Addressee Arg1:T352 Arg2:T380	
T381	Quotation 73899 74487	"Aha! Talk about trying to cure warts with spunk-water such a blame fool way as that! Why, that ain't a-going to do any good. You got to go all by yourself, to the middle of the woods, where you know there's a spunk-water stump, and just as it's midnight you back up against the stump and jam your hand in and say: 'Barley-corn, barley-corn, injun-meal shorts, Spunk-water, spunk-water, swaller these warts,' and then walk away quick, eleven steps, with your eyes shut, and then turn around three times and walk home without speaking to anybody.  Because if you speak the charm's busted."
R487	Addressee Arg1:T350 Arg2:T381	
R488	Speaker Arg1:T352 Arg2:T381	
T382	Quotation 74488 74564	"Well, that sounds like a good way; but that ain't the way Bob Tanner done."
R489	Speaker Arg1:T350 Arg2:T382	
R490	Addressee Arg1:T352 Arg2:T382	
T383	Quotation 74565 74890	"No, sir, you can bet he didn't, becuz he's the wartiest boy in this town; and he wouldn't have a wart on him if he'd knowed how to work spunk-water. I've took off thousands of warts off of my hands that way, Huck. I play with frogs so much that I've always got considerable many warts. Sometimes I take 'em off with a bean."
R491	Addressee Arg1:T350 Arg2:T383	
R492	Speaker Arg1:T352 Arg2:T383	
T384	Quotation 74891 74926	"Yes, bean's good. I've done that."
R493	Speaker Arg1:T350 Arg2:T384	
R494	Addressee Arg1:T352 Arg2:T384	
T385	Quotation 74927 74955	"Have you? What's your way?"
R495	Addressee Arg1:T350 Arg2:T385	
R496	Speaker Arg1:T352 Arg2:T385	
T386	Quotation 74956 75410	"You take and split the bean, and cut the wart so as to get some blood, and then you put the blood on one piece of the bean and take and dig a hole and bury it 'bout midnight at the crossroads in the dark of the moon, and then you burn up the rest of the bean. You see that piece that's got the blood on it will keep drawing and drawing, trying to fetch the other piece to it, and so that helps the blood to draw the wart, and pretty soon off she comes."
R497	Speaker Arg1:T350 Arg2:T386	
R498	Addressee Arg1:T352 Arg2:T386	
T387	Quotation 75411 75684	"Yes, that's it, Huck-- that's it; though when you're burying it if you say 'Down bean; off wart; come no more to bother me!' it's better. That's the way Joe Harper does, and he's been nearly to Coonville and most everywheres. But say-- how do you cure 'em with dead cats?"
R499	Speaker Arg1:T350 Arg2:T387	
R500	Addressee Arg1:T352 Arg2:T387	
T388	Quotation 75685 76145	"Why, you take your cat and go and get in the graveyard 'long about midnight when somebody that was wicked has been buried; and when it's midnight a devil will come, or maybe two or three, but you can't see 'em, you can only hear something like the wind, or maybe hear 'em talk; and when they're taking that feller away, you heave your cat after 'em and say, 'Devil follow corpse, cat follow devil, warts follow cat, I'm done with ye!' That'll fetch ANY wart."
R501	Speaker Arg1:T352 Arg2:T388	
R502	Addressee Arg1:T350 Arg2:T388	
T389	Quotation 76146 76186	"Sounds right. D'you ever try it, Huck?"
R503	Speaker Arg1:T350 Arg2:T389	
R504	Addressee Arg1:T352 Arg2:T389	
T390	Quotation 76187 76224	"No, but old Mother Hopkins told me."
R505	Addressee Arg1:T350 Arg2:T390	
R506	Speaker Arg1:T352 Arg2:T390	
T391	Quotation 76225 76286	"Well, I reckon it's so, then. Becuz they say she's a witch."
R507	Speaker Arg1:T350 Arg2:T391	
R508	Addressee Arg1:T352 Arg2:T391	
T392	Quotation 76287 76575	"Say! Why, Tom, I KNOW she is. She witched pap. Pap says so his own self. He come along one day, and he see she was a-witching him, so he took up a rock, and if she hadn't dodged, he'd a got her. Well, that very night he rolled off'n a shed wher' he was a layin drunk, and broke his arm."
R509	Addressee Arg1:T350 Arg2:T392	
R510	Speaker Arg1:T352 Arg2:T392	
T393	Quotation 76576 76636	"Why, that's awful. How did he know she was a-witching him?"
R511	Speaker Arg1:T350 Arg2:T393	
R512	Addressee Arg1:T352 Arg2:T393	
T394	Quotation 76637 76833	"Lord, pap can tell, easy. Pap says when they keep looking at you right stiddy, they're a-witching you. Specially if they mumble. Becuz when they mumble they're saying the Lord's Prayer backards."
R513	Addressee Arg1:T350 Arg2:T394	
R514	Speaker Arg1:T352 Arg2:T394	
T395	Quotation 76834 76878	"Say, Hucky, when you going to try the cat?"
R515	Speaker Arg1:T350 Arg2:T395	
R516	Addressee Arg1:T352 Arg2:T395	
T396	Quotation 76879 76946	"To-night. I reckon they'll come after old Hoss Williams to-night."
R517	Speaker Arg1:T352 Arg2:T396	
R518	Addressee Arg1:T350 Arg2:T396	
T397	Quotation 76947 77014	"But they buried him Saturday. Didn't they get him Saturday night?"
R519	Speaker Arg1:T350 Arg2:T397	
R520	Addressee Arg1:T352 Arg2:T397	
T398	Quotation 77015 77162	"Why, how you talk! How could their charms work till midnight?-- and THEN it's Sunday. Devils don't slosh around much of a Sunday, I don't reckon."
R521	Speaker Arg1:T352 Arg2:T398	
R522	Addressee Arg1:T350 Arg2:T398	
T399	Quotation 77163 77219	"I never thought of that. That's so. Lemme go with you?"
R523	Speaker Arg1:T350 Arg2:T399	
R524	Addressee Arg1:T352 Arg2:T399	
T400	Quotation 77220 77254	"Of course-- if you ain't afeard."
R525	Addressee Arg1:T350 Arg2:T400	
R526	Speaker Arg1:T352 Arg2:T400	
T401	Quotation 77255 77295	"Afeard! 'Tain't likely. Will you meow?"
R527	Speaker Arg1:T350 Arg2:T401	
R528	Addressee Arg1:T352 Arg2:T401	
T402	Quotation 77296 77515	"Yes-- and you meow back, if you get a chance. Last time, you kep' me a-meowing around till old Hays went to throwing rocks at me and says 'Dern that cat!' and so I hove a brick through his window-- but don't you tell."
R529	Addressee Arg1:T350 Arg2:T402	
R530	Speaker Arg1:T352 Arg2:T402	
T403	Quotation 77516 77628	"I won't. I couldn't meow that night, becuz auntie was watching me, but I'll meow this time. Say-- what's that?"
R531	Speaker Arg1:T350 Arg2:T403	
R532	Addressee Arg1:T352 Arg2:T403	
T404	Quotation 77629 77650	"Nothing but a tick."
R533	Speaker Arg1:T352 Arg2:T404	
R534	Addressee Arg1:T350 Arg2:T404	
T405	Quotation 77651 77673	"Where'd you get him?"
R535	Speaker Arg1:T350 Arg2:T405	
R536	Addressee Arg1:T352 Arg2:T405	
T406	Quotation 77674 77693	"Out in the woods."
R537	Speaker Arg1:T352 Arg2:T406	
R538	Addressee Arg1:T350 Arg2:T406	
T407	Quotation 77694 77721	"What'll you take for him?"
R539	Speaker Arg1:T350 Arg2:T407	
R540	Addressee Arg1:T352 Arg2:T407	
T408	Quotation 77722 77763	"I don't know. I don't want to sell him."
R541	Speaker Arg1:T352 Arg2:T408	
R542	Addressee Arg1:T350 Arg2:T408	
T409	Quotation 77764 77810	"All right. It's a mighty small tick, anyway."
R543	Speaker Arg1:T350 Arg2:T409	
R544	Addressee Arg1:T352 Arg2:T409	
T410	Quotation 77811 77926	"Oh, anybody can run a tick down that don't belong to them. I'm satisfied with it. It's a good enough tick for me."
R545	Speaker Arg1:T352 Arg2:T410	
R546	Addressee Arg1:T350 Arg2:T410	
T411	Quotation 77927 78004	"Sho, there's ticks a plenty. I could have a thousand of 'em if I wanted to."
R547	Speaker Arg1:T350 Arg2:T411	
R548	Addressee Arg1:T352 Arg2:T411	
T412	Quotation 78005 78144	"Well, why don't you? Becuz you know mighty well you can't. This is a pretty early tick, I reckon. It's the first one I've seen this year."
R549	Addressee Arg1:T350 Arg2:T412	
R550	Speaker Arg1:T352 Arg2:T412	
T413	Quotation 78145 78190	"Say, Huck-- I'll give you my tooth for him."
T414	Quotation 78191 78205	"Less see it."
T415	Entity 78206 78209	Tom
T416	Entity 78260 78271	Huckleberry
